A||||22||artherd||Holley Commander 950.  And TEC-II.  DING!||||3||
Z||000000||artherd||12-20-2001||01:34 AM||||I was all set to go with the 950 Commander as modified by Alan Johnson/aka chrfab.   Then I discovered that it aparently converts the motor to batch fire...  and it's knock sensor input needs more mods to work with the N* anyway. (simple, suitable gain op-amp, and a hi/lo/notch filter set at the right width and point, but still.)<P>Batch fire?! Come-on!!  Now, granted, many engines, (the Fiero is one) actually switch from SMPFI (Sequential Multiport Fuel Injection) to batch-fire at around 3,000rpm or so, and with a 4-cam v8 that revs to 6750 STOCK, I don't plan to be spending much time below three grand...<P>But Jesus, Batch-fire is straight out of the pre-electronic days!<P>So now I'm thinking of an Electromotive TEC-II unit, which is even more expensive, but has the advantage of being a much more common, known quantity in engine management. (It will do anything, it'll cook eggs if you gave eggs an output and made the software cook them at 7grand. At least that's been my expierence with them.  I've never touched a 950.)<P><BR>Anyway, this thread is for discussions of aftermarket ECUs for hoped-up cam-crazy Northstars!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000001||DRH||12-20-2001||01:54 AM||||Actually the 2.8 Fiero is batch fire all the time. Most 80s and early 90s cars are, so it's not really that ancient  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>From what I've read, sequential fire is mostly for fuel economy. I think Holley's market is more to people replacing carbs. Even batch fire is a big improvement.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> And, like you mentioned it lets them sell them cheaper too.<P>If you want to go all out, get one with a wide bad O2 sensor. No messing with fuel tables, you just tell it what A/F ratio you want under different conditions, including WOT. Pretty expensive though... I believe wide band is an option on the TECs.<P>Personally, I think I'm going to go the hacked GM ECM route. Cheaper, definitely a lot more involved, but it does sound like an interesting challenge.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Joe Torma||12-20-2001||02:10 AM||||My eggs are ready!  The TECII is quite the impressive unit.<P>In its versions, 4,6,8 cylinders actually fires a pair of injectors.  For the 60* motors, 1&3, 2&5, 3&6.<P>Someday I will master this thing...yeah right.  I do have to upgrade it, wich will allow much more tuning on the fly.<P>Once you have the motor running decently, I think you just have to drive it!  Watch all the sensors, and datalog the drive.  See where the timing is, watch the knocks and adjust.  A dyno would be the perfect test drive of course.<P>Just get the TECII and hook the GPO(general purpose output) to your electric skillet and 7grand eggs-to-go. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I think I'm up to version "G" of my 3400program.  Make some changes, save to different filename and upload.  If it is worse, load the old data back onto it.<P>The neat thing to do, and will be better with the upgrade, is to change like the timing from the MONITOR ENGINE FUNCTION screen and hit enter--the engine instantly responds and runs different!<P><P>------------------<BR>Its never going to be finished...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig2_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||artherd||12-20-2001||04:00 AM||||Hi Joe, sounds like you're having some good 'ol hardcore *development* fun!   I really do like the TEC products, from what expierence I've had with them (read: dorking around other people's cars)<P><BR>Hrm, been reading more about the 950 Commander, and it is looking better...<BR> <A HREF="http://www.holley.com/HiOctn/ProdLine/Products/C950/C950MPFI/534-120.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.holley.com/HiOctn/ProdLine/Products/C950/C950MPFI/534-120.html</A> <P>It's realtime ajustable (Like Joe's TEC-II when he gets around to upgrading it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> As in you can tweak things, and the engine responds NOW, while running. no shuting down to load new code, then start and test again.<P>And the software looks fairly comprable:  <IMG SRC="http://www.holley.com/HiOctn/ProdLine/Products/C950/fFuelGrph.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.holley.com/HiOctn/ProdLine/Products/C950/fFuelMap.jpg"> <P>...even if the dam thing IS batch-fire :S)<P>Hey, I think it even has an egg-cooker output! :S)<P>I'll probally go with whichever ECU Alan Johnson recomends (I eventually will have him  install his solid lifters, new valve springs, and do some head work.  Suddenly this gives me over 400bhp... and a redline somewhere in the 8-9grand area... eeep!!)<P>Oh yeah, the commander 950 is $1300 with wiring harness, complete.<P>Wheras the TEC-II starts at around $1900, then I still need a bunch of junk.<P>Just have the 950 knock sensor to make work (should be simple, think Alan will give me a discount if I make him 100 of these?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000004||Joe Torma||12-20-2001||08:43 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Oh yeah, the commander 950 is $1300 with wiring harness, complete.<P>Wheras the TEC-II starts at around $1900, then I still need a bunch of junk.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You sure about the price on that TEC?  Sounds like someone's marking that up a lot.<BR>Dunno, maybe it gotten more 'spensive.  Call Electromotive direct.  When I ordered, it was $1600 w/o sensors...your caddy is new enough, you don't need them.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||VISCERAL||12-20-2001||09:37 AM||||What about going with a Haltech or ACCEL DFI setup?||||reg||1
Z||000006||gargoyle||12-20-2001||12:38 PM||||Screw the electronics and just bolt on a set of Webers.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||BigTurbo87GT||12-20-2001||06:49 PM||||Good grief, all my hopes are tied up in the 950 being perfect.<BR>Ben, stop scaring me :-D<BR>I'll buy one of them-there knock sensor do-hickies. If you make it I will buy, I'm one heck of a consumer.<BR>I would have thought CHR would provide a knock interface as they do the timing interface.<BR>Oh well, my single vendor dream isn't shattered just slightly fractured. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||wcapman||12-20-2001||07:27 PM||||Hi.  I'm running an OBD1 right now on my N*/Getrag.  Have since 1998.  But I'm planning on installing Alan's cams soon, hence the Commander 950.  A few words about that.<P>Don't worry about Batch fire.  In fact it is more performance oriented than sequential.  You'll only add a few more pounds of emissions.  What you will miss is the EGR and carbon canister purge solenoid as the Holley won't run either one.  I don't consider that a big deal.<P>Alan said that the DIS for a N* is radically different on timing than any other GM.  He has to change the firmware in the Holley to make it work.  Actually I have a friend who works for Holley, and THEY make the change for Alan,  BUT  they will not sell it to me or anyone.  You must get it from Alan.  I already had a 950, so Alan "fixed" it for me when I bought the cams, for a fee, of course.  I figure he has done all the work, he should get paid for it.<P>The bottom line is it is actually very difficult to program one of these aftermarket computers.  Alan has a program that is already set up for the N*, that only requires a little tweaking.  The EASIEST thing to do is go with Alan's setup.  Does anyone know if there is a running N* on a TECII or other system?  That would be good to know.||||reg||1
Z||000009||artherd||12-21-2001||06:44 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by wcapman:<BR><B>Alan said that the DIS for a N* is radically different on timing than any other GM.  He has to change the firmware in the Holley to make it work. ... I figure he has done all the work, he should get paid for it.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Agreed, I'm 100% into supporting the creator's R&D here (plus, I want him to be my friend when he does my cams/heads/lifters too, you lucky dog  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I belive Alan/chrfab also sells TEC-IIs for the Northstars (have to use an external 'crank' timing wheel that actually gets mounted on the waterpump cam-takeoff drive.<P>Several pics of his clients engines with those wheels, so I'll assume it's running...<P><BR>On the knock sensor: they're really not that critical, a *properly* running engine should never need them.  But things wear, get out of spec, gaskets leak... (idoits put regular gas in.......)   Knock sensors will save your motor from these travesties.  Otherwise detonation on a 10.3:1 aluminum engine has this nasty tendency to melt *everything*...<P>So long and short of it is, I'll install it all sans-sensor.  But I'll work on adapting it too!!<P><BR>Batch-fire still dosen't leave me with warm fuzzy feelings, but I got my brakes new today, so I'm in a good mood anyway  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Guess I'll just rev the snot out of it all the time, which is what I've planned anyway!<P><BR>Joe: I think the v8 version of the TEC-II is probally a few bucks more, plus this was with some sensors (needed, the N* is new, but *funky*  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>Best!<BR>Ben. <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000010||Joe Torma||12-21-2001||09:47 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B> <BR>Joe: I think the v8 version of the TEC-II is probally a few bucks more, plus this was with some sensors (needed, the N* is new, but *funky*   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Gotta know...what's different about the N*(don't know anything about them)?||||reg||1
Z||000011||BigTurbo87GT||12-21-2001||10:57 AM||||With regard to the electronics the N* has both crank sensors and cam sensors.<BR>(Ben or somebody who else who knows more about northstars will have to correct my mistake from here on)<BR>The timing system is different in that the cam sensors don't generate a pulse signal that anything seems to be able to interprate for timing (other than stock ecu) AND the crank sensors provide feekback (not used for timing) to determine misfire/smoothness of engine (very slight difference between cam reference position and crank reference position means a cylinder misfired).<BR>I believe the cam timing signal is half of a normal crank revolution but I haven't looked very far into that as I expect to use the pre-fab CHR 950 unit.<P>With regard to engine in general, geez, everything is different  :-D||||reg||1
Z||000012||Will||12-21-2001||11:42 AM||||Well, not quite.<BR>The cam position sensor hits once every other crank revolution. It's not useful for anything but knowing where TDC #1 is.<BR>OBDI systems don't have any misfire detection. That's OBDII.<P>What everyone and his brother seems to overlook is that the Cadillac ignition brick takes the analog pulses from the CKS and CPS and converts them to easily readable digital pulses: 24x blip to low for the CKS, and 1/2x 50% DC for the CPS (the signal is high for one crank revolution, low for the next). The Aurora FSM has a diagram spelling out the nature of these pulse trains clearly, if not is exacting detail. Anyway, the CPS changeover is synchronized with the #1 CKS pulse, the trailing edge of which is 10 degrees ATDC for cylinders 1/4 (N* firing order is cast into right cam cover: 1,2,7,3,4,5,6,8).<P>In conclusion, the analog signals may be wierd, but the digital signals are straight forward standard GM.||||reg||1
Z||000013||DRH||12-21-2001||12:12 PM||||Ben,<P>I noticed someone mentioned the Holley doesn't support EGR. That's currently not a problem for those of us living in free states  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> (although that could change), but what about people with emissions testing? I noticed you're in CA, how are you planning on getting the Holley, or any other aftermarket ECM, past your friendly state guys?<P>David.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Joe Torma||12-21-2001||01:19 PM||||With the TEC II you have no need for the factory sensors(I had the above mentioned sensors on the 3400).<P>You just have to put their trigger wheel on.  Is threre something else?  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||zeelebuba||12-21-2001||03:13 PM||||Check out <A HREF="http://www.sdsefi.com/." TARGET=_blank>http://www.sdsefi.com/.</A> <P> Im looked at accel/holly and decided to go with these guys- Was lots cheaper in canada||||reg||1
Z||000016||artherd||12-21-2001||08:09 PM||||Hey will, any thoughts on a 180degree header setup for the Northstar firing pattern? It's giving me a serious headache, guess I really need to pull an oil pan and just look at the bottom end.  Requires effort though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>I'm going to have to register the car in Nevada; the People's Republic of California just won't approve *ANY* manual transmissioned Northstar powered car.  Peroid (Because Cadilac NEVER made a standard-shift N* car.)<P>It's a pity. (Note that the Electromotive/Holley/etc computers will basically be by defenition emmisions-illegal in all of CA on *any* controled vehicles.)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DRH:<BR><B>Ben,<P>I noticed someone mentioned the Holley doesn't support EGR. That's currently not a problem for those of us living in free states   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> (although that could change), but what about people with emissions testing? I noticed you're in CA, how are you planning on getting the Holley, or any other aftermarket ECM, past your friendly state guys?<P>David.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000017||86 FIERO GT||12-21-2001||10:37 PM||||Just to correct yall, the northstar is capable and rev limited to 7200 rpms, that is when it turns on the service engine soon light and sets a code.  Its been done and proven. An OBDII engine can be manipulated too to think its lean and dump more fuel to make more power at high rpms or you can hook up a toggle switch that can let you switch it to batch dump or normal running system.<P>------------------<BR>86 Fiero GT , soon to be N*ed and worked over.||||reg||1
Z||000018||wcapman||12-22-2001||10:10 AM||||My N* chip has the rev limiter set at 6200.  It's an OBD1.  I understand that the 275 hp engines were set at 6200 and the 300 at 6800.  At least the chips I have played with were such.  This makes sense because the vlave springs aren't as strong in the 275 hp versions.  Alan Johnson says if you ever float a valve the engine is history.<P>You can make some diddle with the OBD1 or 2 my wiring in a variable resistor with the engine temp sender.  By tricking the engine to think it is colder than it is it will richen up the fuel curve slightly.||||reg||1
Z||000019||wcapman||12-23-2001||11:50 AM||||Just met with my friend from Holley.  He's home for the holidays.  Took him for a ride in my car.  He had only seen it on jack stands before.  I think he was inmpressed, although he could have just been polite.  His normal ride is a 450hp vette.  No comparisons made.<P>Anyway, Holley has a new version of the Commander 950.  It has some hardware changes that add a lot of data logging functions and some other things. Still will not run the emissions.<P>My friend says he would be willing to get a few more 950's at his cost, about $600 if anyone is interested.  It will still not run a N* without Alan's conversion, which Holley does for Alan but will not pass along to anyone else.<P>I also asked about the knock sensor but was promised that would be looked into and he would get back to me later.  Anyone interested?||||reg||1
Z||000020||artherd||12-26-2001||04:33 AM||||Just talked to Alan (via email), and unless I misread him entirely, his 950 is (now?) prepared to use the stock N* knock sensor... (to set max timing advance under all conditions.)<P>You have PM on the Holley  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> (I'm still into supporting Alan's R&D, but can't ignore parts at cost!  Maybe I'll do both, by buying lots of cams/lifter/springs from Alan.)<P><BR>Mmmm 365bhp....<P>Merry X-mas!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">      <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red.                     "Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver, In-Progreess.                                        -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
